  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show an \nelongated area of surface low pressure stretching from Lake Erie \ndown into the Ohio Valley. Cyclonic flow around this system \ncontinues to maintain low stratus along the Lake Michigan \nshoreline, while fair-weather cumulus has developed inland with \nafternoon heating and residual low-level moisture over northeast \nWisconsin. Meanwhile, a surface high-pressure ridge centered over \nOntario is expanding southward into the Upper Mississippi Valley \nand western Great Lakes. Across the broader national picture, an \nupper-level shortwave trough over the Northern High Plains will \ntrack eastward over the next 24 to 48 hours, serving as the \ncatalyst for our next system mid-week.\n\nTonight Through Monday Night...\nAs high pressure continues to push into Wisconsin this afternoon \nand evening, drier low-level air will advect into the state. \nHeating and dry advection will lift cloud bases before skies clear\nearly this evening as the center of the surface high settles \ndirectly over the area. Excellent radiational cooling conditions \nunder clear skies and light winds will favor ground fog \ndevelopment after midnight through 8 to 9 AM Monday. While \noperational model guidance continues to underplay fog potential, \nupstream surface observations across northeast Minnesota and the \nwestern Upper Peninsula showed widespread ground fog early this \nmorning under similar conditions. Have maintained patchy to areas \nof fog in the forecast for north-central and far northeast \nWisconsin late tonight. Any fog will burn off rapidly after \nsunrise Monday, giving way to abundant sunshine and comfortable \nhumidity. Afternoon high temperatures on Monday will reach the low\nto mid 80s inland, holding in the mid to upper 70s near Lake \nMichigan. Dry and quiet conditions persist Monday night under high\npressure.\n\nTuesday Through Wednesday Morning...\nThe upper-level flow pattern transitions to nearly zonal across \nthe northern tier of the CONUS, with flattened troughing over the \nnorthern Plains. A cold front will approach from the west on \nTuesday, bringing the next widespread chance of showers and \nthunderstorms. Frontal convergence appears to weaken as the \nboundary moves into central and eastern Wisconsin late Tuesday \nmorning and afternoon. Forecast soundings show a stout capping \ninversion in the 800 to 700 mb layer, suggesting much of the \nprecipitation through Tuesday evening will be post-frontal or \nelevated. Modest instability with Most Unstable CAPE values of 500\nto 1000 J/kg and deep-layer shear peaking around 25 to 30 knots \nsupport only a marginal threat for an isolated severe storm. \n\nA more impactful scenario appears to be setting up late Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning. Models show a stronger consensus on \nmid-level frontogenesis strengthening on the nose of a developing \nlow-level jet over Iowa. This is expected to organize a mesoscale \nconvective complex that tracks northeastward directly across \nnortheast Wisconsin. While severe weather parameters remain \nmodest, heavy rainfall looks to be the main threat. Precipitable \nwater values of 140 to 150 percent of normal combined with deep-\nlayer flow parallel to the boundary raise the potential for \nlocalized training and a low risk of urban or small-stream \nflooding. Rain chances will diminish from west to east Wednesday \nafternoon as the complex exits.\n\nThursday Through Sunday...\nPredictability decreases significantly heading into the late week\nand weekend. The slow-moving, flattened upper-level trough will\ngradually slide across the Great Lakes region from Friday through\nSunday. Periodic shortwaves embedded in the flow will generate\nshower and thunderstorm opportunities, though exact timing remains\nuncertain this far out. Temperatures and humidity levels are\nexpected to hover right around normal seasonal benchmarks through\nthe extended period, with daily highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s\nand overnight lows in the 50s to low 60s.",
